---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Demo from '../components/Demo';
import { Mascot } from '../components/Mascot';
---

<Base
  title="Swaprika, a GraphQL API for ingredient substitution that knows the context"
  description="What can replace this ingredient, in this situation, and what changes if you do. A GraphQL API returning ratios, what the swap loses, the adjustments it needs, and the contexts where it fails."
  page="home"
>
  <main>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>Butter in a cake is not<br /> butter in a croissant.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Swaprika is a GraphQL API for ingredient substitution. It answers what can
          replace an ingredient <em>in a specific situation</em>, what the swap gives
          up, and what you have to change to make it work.
        </p>
        <div class="cta">
          <a class="btn primary" href="#demo">Try it, no signup</a>
          <a class="btn" href="/docs">Read the docs</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-art">
        <Mascot client:load mood="idle" size={230} />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="eyebrow">The problem</div>
      <h2>A substitution list with no context is a support ticket waiting to happen.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        This is the failure every recipe app with a dietary filter eventually ships.
      </p>
      <div class="story">
        <div class="step">
          <span class="n">1</span>
          <p>
            A user with a dairy allergy opens your app and asks what to use instead of
            butter. Your backend calls a substitution endpoint.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <span class="n">2</span>
          <p>
            It returns a flat list: coconut oil 1:1, applesauce 1:2, olive oil 1:1. No
            context, no conditions. Your app shows the list. It looks correct.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="step bad">
          <span class="n">3</span>
          <p>
            The user was making croissants. Coconut oil cannot laminate. The dough fails,
            and the person blames your app, not the API you bought.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="eyebrow">The difference</div>
      <h2>Same question. Two answers.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Both of these answer "what replaces butter". Only one is safe to put in front of
        a user who has not told you what they are cooking.
      </p>

      <div class="compare">
        <div class="col">
          <h4>A flat substitution endpoint</h4>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "ingredient": "butter",
  "substitutes": [
    "coconut oil (1:1)",
    "greek yogurt (1:2)",
    "avocado (1:1)",
    "applesauce (1:2)"
  ]
}`}</code></pre>
          <p class="verdict">
            Four suggestions, no conditions. <b>Fine for a muffin, wrong for a croissant,
            wrong for buttercream</b>, and nothing in the payload tells you which is
            which.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="col ours">
          <h4>Swaprika</h4>
          <pre><code>{`{
  "node": "Neutral oil",
  "ratio": { "amount": 0.8, "basis": "WEIGHT",
    "note": "Butter is ~80% fat, 16% water." },
  "preservesRoles": ["FAT"],
  "losesRoles": ["CREAMED_AIR", "WATER_CONTENT"],
  "effects": [
    { "dimension": "RISE", "direction": "LESS" }
  ],
  "adjustments": [
    { "action": "ADD_LIQUID",
      "amount": "~15% of the butter weight" }
  ],
  "validFor": {
    "excludedTechniques": ["CREAMING", "LAMINATION"]
  },
  "reliability": "ESTABLISHED"
}`}</code></pre>
          <p class="verdict">
            <b>A ratio with a basis</b>, because 0.8 by weight is not 0.8 by volume.
            <b>What it loses.</b> <b>What to change.</b> <b>Where it fails.</b>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="demo">
      <div class="eyebrow">Try it</div>
      <h2>Change the situation. Watch the answer change.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        This runs against the live API, with a free key by default. Ask for butter in a
        croissant and you get nothing back, because nothing here has been verified to
        survive lamination. Returning nothing is the feature.
      </p>
      <Demo client:visible />
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="eyebrow">Who this is for</div>
      <h2>Four places this goes wrong today.</h2>
      <div class="audience">
        <div class="who">
          <h3>Recipe and meal planning apps</h3>
          <p>
            You already filter by diet. The moment a user swaps an ingredient, that
            filter is only as good as the substitution data behind it.
          </p>
          <span class="need">Needs: dietary constraints, ratios, failure cases</span>
        </div>
        <div class="who">
          <h3>Allergy and intolerance tools</h3>
          <p>
            A swap that removes dairy but introduces soy or tree nut is not a safe
            answer. Every edge declares what it introduces, not only what it removes.
          </p>
          <span class="need">Needs: introduced allergens, not just satisfied ones</span>
        </div>
        <div class="who">
          <h3>AI cooking assistants</h3>
          <p>
            A language model will confidently invent a ratio. Grounding it against a
            structured source with an explicit reliability level and recorded failures is
            cheaper than apologising afterwards.
          </p>
          <span class="need">Needs: structured facts, provenance, reliability</span>
        </div>
        <div class="who">
          <h3>Grocery and out of stock flows</h3>
          <p>
            When an item is unavailable, the replacement has to work for what the
            customer is actually cooking, not merely be nutritionally similar.
          </p>
          <span class="need">Needs: context aware swaps, regional naming</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="eyebrow">Plans</div>
      <h2>The price list is the schema.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        Paid fields are declared on the field itself with an auth scope. A free key does
        not get a truncated copy of the response. It gets an explicit authorization error
        on exactly the fields it is not entitled to, and everything else still resolves.
        Flip the toggle in the demo above and watch it happen.
      </p>
      <div class="plans">
        <div class="plan">
          <h3>Free</h3>
          <p class="price">No signup during the preview</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Ingredients, taxonomy, regional names</li>
            <li>Substitutions with ratio and basis</li>
            <li>What each swap keeps and loses</li>
            <li>Dietary impact, including introduced allergens</li>
            <li>Which contexts are answerable at all</li>
            <li class="no">Effects, adjustments, reliability</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="plan featured">
          <h3>Developer</h3>
          <p class="price">Pricing not live yet</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Everything in Free</li>
            <li>Effects: flavour, texture, rise, browning</li>
            <li>Adjustments, the compensating actions</li>
            <li>Reliability of each claim</li>
            <li>Full validity and exclusion lists</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="plan">
          <h3>Pro</h3>
          <p class="price">Pricing not live yet</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Everything in Developer</li>
            <li>Sources behind each claim</li>
            <li>Higher query complexity budget</li>
            <li>Commercial data licence</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="eyebrow">Status</div>
      <h2>Where this actually is, honestly.</h2>
      <p class="sub">
        An early and deliberately small dataset. Every edge is hand written and chosen
        because context changes the answer. It is not a scrape, and it is not pretending
        to be complete.
      </p>
      <div class="facts">
        <div class="fact"><b>45</b><span>ingredients and categories</span></div>
        <div class="fact"><b>21</b><span>substitution facts</span></div>
        <div class="fact"><b>28</b><span>recorded failure cases</span></div>
        <div class="fact"><b>0</b><span>scraped rows</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <b>What it does not cover yet:</b> broad ingredient coverage, branded products,
        full nutrition, and most cuisines outside Europe and North America. If you ask
        for a context that has not been tested, the API says so instead of guessing.
        That is the one promise this project makes.
      </div>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/docs">Read the docs</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/graphql">Open the playground</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
